1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,380
Well, my group has been doing a project on how household waste is recycled in Britain.

2
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:15,500
We were quite shocked to discover that only 9% of people here in the UK make an effort to recycle their household waste.

3
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:19,364
This is a lower figure than in most other European countries,

4
00:00:19,364 --> 00:00:23,200
and needs to increase dramatically in the next few years

5
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:26,390
if the government is going to meet its recycling targets.

6
00:00:26,390 --> 00:00:37,910
The agreed targets for the UK mean that by 2008 we must reduce our carbon dioxide emissions by 12.5%, compared with 1990.

7
00:00:37,910 --> 00:00:43,919
And recycling can help to achieve that goal, in two main ways:

8
00:00:43,919 --> 00:00:46,640
the production of recycled glass

9
00:00:46,640 --> 00:00:52,431
and paper uses much less energy than producing them from virgin materials,

10
00:00:52,431 --> 00:00:58,940
and also recycling reduces greenhouse gas emissions from landfill sites and incineration plants.

11
00:00:58,940 --> 00:01:01,780
As part of our project

12
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:07,122
we carried out a survey of people in the street, and the thing that came up over

13
00:01:07,122 --> 00:01:12,700
and over again is that people don't think it's easy enough to recycle their waste.

14
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:15,838
One problem is that there aren't enough 'drop-off' sites,

15
00:01:15,838 --> 00:01:20,370
that is, the places where the public are supposed to take their waste.

16
00:01:20,370 --> 00:01:27,519
We also discovered that waste that's collected from householders is taken to places called 'bring banks',

17
00:01:27,519 --> 00:01:30,200
for sorting and baling into loads.

18
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:36,770
One problem here is taking out everything that shouldn't have been placed in the recycling containers:

19
00:01:36,770 --> 00:01:40,220
people put all sorts of things into bottle banks

20
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:43,340
like plastic bags and even broken umbrellas.

21
00:01:43,340 --> 00:01:46,250
All this has to be removed by hand.

22
00:01:46,250 --> 00:01:53,546
Another difficulty is that toughened glass used for cooking doesn't fully melt at the temperature required for other glass,

23
00:01:53,546 --> 00:01:57,770
and so that also has to be picked out by hand.

24
00:01:57,770 --> 00:02:02,480
Glass is easy to recycle because it can be reused over

25
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:04,560
and over again without becoming weaker.

26
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:09,120
Two million tons of glass is thrown away each year

27
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:15,355
that is, seven billion bottles and jars;

28
00:02:15,355 --> 00:02:22,340
but only 500,000 tons of that is collected and recycled.

29
00:02:22,340 --> 00:02:25,961
Oddly enough, half the glass that's collected is green,

30
00:02:25,961 --> 00:02:32,800
and a lot of that is imported, so more green glass is recycled than the UK needs.

31
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:38,510
As a result, new uses are being developed for recycled glass,

32
00:02:38,510 --> 00:02:40,060
particularly green glass, for example

33
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:44,220
in fibreglass manufacture and water filtration.

34
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:49,300
A company called CLF Aggregates makes a product for roads

35
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:52,310
and 30% of the material is crushed glass.

36
00:02:52,310 --> 00:03:01,570
For recycling paper, Britain comes second in Europe with 40%, behind Germany's amazing 70%.

37
00:03:01,570 --> 00:03:05,051
When recycling started, there were quality problems,

38
00:03:05,051 --> 00:03:10,520
so it was difficult to use recycled paper in office printers.

39
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:13,592
But these problems have now been solved,

40
00:03:13,592 --> 00:03:15,620
and Martin's, based in South London

41
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:20,614
produces a range of office stationery which is 100% recycled,

42
00:03:20,614 --> 00:03:25,880
costs the same as normal paper and is of equally high quality.

43
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:32,030
But this high quality comes at a cost in terms of the waste produced during the process.

44
00:03:32,030 --> 00:03:36,914
Over a third of the waste paper that comes in can't be used in the recycled paper,

45
00:03:36,914 --> 00:03:39,500
leaving the question of what to do with it.

46
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:44,569
One firm, Papersave, currently sells this to farmers as a soil conditioner,

47
00:03:44,569 --> 00:03:50,483
though this practice will soon be banned because of transport costs and the smell,

48
00:03:50,483 --> 00:03:55,130
and the company is looking into the possibility of alternative uses.

49
00:03:55,130 --> 00:03:58,960
Plastic causes problems, because there are

50
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:00,880
so many different types of plastic in use today,

51
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:04,330
and each one has to be dealt with differently.

52
00:04:04,330 --> 00:04:08,730
Pacrite recycles all sorts of things, from bottles to car bumpers,

53
00:04:08,730 --> 00:04:14,330
and one of its most successful activities is recycling plastic bottles to make containers

54
00:04:14,330 --> 00:04:18,330
which are used all over the country to collect waste.

55
00:04:18,330 --> 00:04:22,440
The Save-a-Cup scheme was set up by the vending

56
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:30,660
and plastics industries to recycle as many as possible of the three, and-a-half billion polystyrene cups used each year.

57
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:35,120
At the moment 500 million polycups are collected

58
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:37,399
processed and sold on to other businesses,

59
00:04:37,399 --> 00:04:42,790
such as Waterford, which turns the cups into pencils, and Johnson & Jones,

60
00:04:42,790 --> 00:04:49,080
a Welsh-based firm, which has developed a wide variety of items, including business cards.

61
00:04:49,080 --> 00:04:57,000
Well, to sum up, there seems to be plenty of research going on into how to re-use materials,

62
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:04,040
but the biggest problem is getting people to think about recycling instead of throwing things away.

63
00:05:04,040 --> 00:05:08,550
At least doing the research made us much more careful.
